WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.110
KENT WEARE: Welcome to the Talk

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.030
to Your Terminal Using
Dataverse Plugin

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.620
for Coding Agent Sessions.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.000
My name is Kent Weare.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.950
I'm a Product Manager
at Microsoft,

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.600
and I'm here joined with --

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.550
SUYESH KSHIRSAGAR: Hi,
my name is Suyash.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.280
I'm Software Engineering
Manager at Microsoft.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.630
Welcome to our session.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:16.890
Thanks for joining.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.290
KENT WEARE: Your business data
already lives in Dataverse,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.150
but today, getting value

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.810
out of it still means navigating
portals, writing queries,

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.390
and filing tickets with
the platform team.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.010
SUYESH KSHIRSAGAR: What
if your developer,

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.600
your operations analyst,

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.960
and your admin could all
describe what they need

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.200
in plain English
and get it done?

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.170
That's what the Dataverse
Skills plugin delivers.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.150
Single capability embedded

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.760
in the coding tools
your team already uses,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.590
which turns your business intent

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.750
into governed
Dataverse operations.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.980
Schemas, data, queries,
security, admin operations.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.290
Everything without
leaving your workflow.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.230
In this session, we're going
to see three different roles,

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:02.160
three completely different jobs,
but using a single approach.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.290
KENT WEARE: The platform
finally moves at the speed

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.570
of the people who depend on it.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.280
SUYESH KSHIRSAGAR: In this
segment, we are going

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.390
to see Maya, a builder,
achieve following objectives.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.190
How can she connect
to Dataverse?

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.160
How can she describe
and build a data model?

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.620
She could also import
some reference data.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.140
And finally, she could
also validate the data she

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.360
just loaded.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.430
KENT WEARE: So Maya's
never touched Dataverse.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:30.150
She doesn't know her org URL,
and she shouldn't have to.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.320
She goes ahead and installs
the Dataverse Skills plugin,

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.540
first in the tool
that she's using,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.620
and connects using her
Microsoft sign-in.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.580
So she goes ahead and creates
a prompt instructing the agent

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.280
to connect me to my
Dataverse environment.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.170
I don't know my org URL,

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.750
and notice she doesn't
have to paste one.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.990
The agent subsequently
discovers her environment

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:59.280
from her Microsoft identity,
configures the MCP server,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.860
and verifies the connection.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.450
All from one prompt, no
config files, no docs,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.910
no setup, no guides required.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.650
Maya's now going to
build a data model,

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:17.940
and will do so by describing the
roast batch tracking system she

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.250
actually needs.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.120
In business terms, not platform
terms, no technical jargon.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.500
One prompt builds the whole data
model, the form, and the view.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.540
The prompt that Maya
will issue includes,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.840
I'm building a roast batch
tracking system for Zava Coffee.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.360
We're a B2B roaster
and distributor.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.040
We need to track our operations.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.330
Here's what I need.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.280
A beans table for our
coffee varieties,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.320
including the
appropriate fields.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.540
A roast batches table,

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.030
which includes a
self-referential lookup

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.930
for a parent batch, so we can
track re-roasts and blends.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.640
A quality checks table,

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.430
which includes a lookup
to the roast batch.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:59.490
An orders table, which includes
a many-to-many relationship

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.650
between roast
batches and orders.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.920
And we'll also need to build
a main form for roast batches

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.710
and a view called
Batches Ready for QA,

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:10.620
which has a filter
status of cupping.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.920
If you can include that as
well, that would be great.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.500
Okay, so we'll also need to go
ahead and create everything

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.070
in a solution called
Zava Coffee Ops.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.590
So Maya's built a
real-world data model

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:27.060
that included sophisticated
features like choices, lookups,

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:30.540
including CEPH referential,
and many-to-many.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.000
This took place all
from one prompt.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.880
Subsequently, the agent
picked the right tool

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.230
for each operation, and we
built a form and view as well.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.390
Maya never needed to
manually click buttons

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.610
in the Maker Portal
to make this work.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:49.570
The app and data model is usable
the moment the agent finishes.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.070
So the scheme is done.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:57.390
Maya now wants to bring in
the team's real spreadsheets

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.160
that contains their
reference data.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:03.540
Four files, four owners,
no goods in any of them.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:08.820
Only business keys, bean codes,
batch numbers, and cafe names.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.920
Maya's prompt will include
instructions on how

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.460
to import the four Excel files
from the reference data folder

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.040
into the tables that
were just created.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.440
Instructions have also
been provided to ensure

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.570
that business keys
are to be used

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.660
to resolve lookups
and never goods.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.770
Should there be any failures,
Maya needs to be informed.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:32.910
So yes, those look to be real
Excel files and not CSV files.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.280
How is the Dataverse coding
agent able to go ahead

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.420
and parse those files?

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:36.840
SUYESH KSHIRSAGAR: Yeah.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:37.830
This is the benefit

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:40.640
of the Dataverse plugin
for coding agents.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.230
It has skills that know how best
to address a particular use case

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:47.840
or task at hand,
including the ability

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.440
to use various tools behind
the scene like the Python SDK

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.450
and to generate on-demand tools

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.570
that help achieve the
user requirements.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.340
KENT WEARE: Wow,
that was awesome.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.220
Looks more like a data pipeline
than a sample generator.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.070
It was cool to see how it
handled the dependencies

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.660
in the lookups.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.930
Maya needs to perform a quick
sanity check before handing off

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:11.130
to Riya.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:13.260
Let's just run a
query to validate

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:17.250
that we do have the expected row
counts from our table queries.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:18.690
Maya will describe how she would

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.000
like to perform a quick
sanity check by asking

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.100
for the row counts
for each table

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.190
and confirm the two
roast batches are linked

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.020
to their parents.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.590
We can now see that the row
counts match the spreadsheets.

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:34.470
Both the roast links
resolved correctly.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.290
Maya now has a
connected working app

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.720
with the team's real data in it.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:41.820
Now, let's go ahead
and shift gears

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.220
and see what Riya is up to.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.860
SUYESH KSHIRSAGAR: Riya is a
DevOps analyst for Zawa Coffee.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.220
And in this segment, we are
going to see how Riya converses

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.500
with CRM in plain English.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.250
Connect to Dataverse, perform
opportunity analysis,

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.430
identify customers who
haven't recently ordered,

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.500
add notes to opportunities,

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.750
and even perform some
task management,

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:06.270
all in natural language.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.250
Riya already lives
in a terminal.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:12.120
She also has the same Dataverse
plugin installed as Maya.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.660
She knows the org URL, but she
doesn't have to give it to.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:20.370
She'll let the agent find that
from her Microsoft sign-in.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.500
Riya will issue a simple prompt.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.090
I have Dataverse plugin.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:26.940
Can you connect me to
my CRM environment?

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:31.140
Now, the plugin is going to do
the discovery of all the tools

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:33.120
that are installed,
the prerequisites

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.140
that her machine has, perform
the auth, and now connect her

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:38.880
to the Dataverse environment.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.440
There was a single prompt.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.960
There was one auth,
one confirmation.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.100
Same install Maya used last
week, but a different job today.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:49.950
Now, the same prompt also
connects her to CRM tables

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.560
like account,
opportunity, contact.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.440
Everything is verified as
part of this connection.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.110
Riya is good to go.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:06:59.730
Now, the real test.

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:03.990
Thursday afternoon, Riya needs
Carlos, the sales manager,

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:05.580
pipeline review materials.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:09.120
She asks the way she would
ask a junior analyst.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.930
No need for her to
write any fetch XML.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.070
Now, the prompt.

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:16.290
Show me Carlos'
open opportunities

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:20.850
over $100,000 closing
this quarter.

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:23.490
This is where the value
of the plugin kicks in.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:25.530
It discovers the
underlying tools

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:28.260
and then uses the skill Dbquery

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:30.990
to embed the knowledge
in a Python script.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:33.510
It runs the Python script,
and this used to take

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:35.340
so much more time before.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.280
Now, we don't have to
worry about goods,

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.870
no estimated close dates, no
state code, just the cafe names,

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:46.650
deal names, dollars,
dates, and stages.

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:49.770
This is the shape of a
new pipeline review.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.440
KENT WEARE: And notice
she said Carlos by name.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:56.640
The agent looked him up in the
system user table automatically

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.400
and scoped the query
to his records.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.380
Named user scoping.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:06.390
Same machinery as my,
just a different anchor.

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:07.680
SUYESH KSHIRSAGAR: Next prompt.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:13.230
Show me cafes in Portland that
haven't reordered in 30 days.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:14.880
Watch this.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.570
This is the one that used
to take Riya 20 minutes

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:25.170
or more using Advanced Find,
Excel, and other ad hoc tooling.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.070
Look here.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.110
Reorder is not a field.

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:29.460
It's a relationship.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:32.970
Plus, there is some
math around date.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:35.580
The agent figured
out reorder maps

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:39.690
to closed one opportunities,
joined the account table

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:41.310
to an opportunity table.

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:47.810
It also computed the gap and
gave Riya a clean call list.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:51.410
This is where her Friday's
accounts to nudge job becomes

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:56.660
so much easier, and it's
all written for her.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.810
Show me open follow-up
tasks due for this week.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:04.110
Now, Riya checks her own
follow-ups for the week.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.290
Same scoping pattern
as Carlos' pipeline.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:09.060
It's just a different anchor.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:14.690
This time, she is interested
in records that she owns.

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:17.900
Here is where the
agent again resolves I

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.920
to Riya's user identity.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.560
Queries the tasks
where she's the owner

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.140
and when the due date
is for this week.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.840
It returns subject, due date,

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:30.990
and regarding records
display name.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.990
Same pattern as
Carlos' pipeline,

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:37.230
just a different anchor.

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:39.240
Remember the coffee
shop from Portland

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.940
who hasn't ordered
in the last 30 days?

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:45.690
Well, Carlos just texted
Riya this morning.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:50.130
He called the customer, but he
has not, of course, logged it.

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:52.260
Riya will take care of it.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:53.840
Here is the prompt.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.350
Add a note to the coffee
shop opportunity.

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:58.850
Carlos called them today.

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:00.500
They're ready to move forward.

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:03.570
Sending the contract Monday.

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:06.690
Here is where the plugin
uses his Dvdata skill

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:09.840
and makes the rights to
the CRM environment.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:11.340
That is quite easy.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:15.540
Much easier than having to find
the opportunity through the UX

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:19.200
and make the updates manually.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:21.840
If we expect agents
to perform well,

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:25.140
we need to ensure we
have good data hygiene.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:29.010
With this in mind, Riya will
clean up a stale follow-up

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.290
on Powell's opportunity.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.060
Mark the follow-up task

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:37.320
on the Powell's
opportunity as complete.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.990
This is the same pattern
we have seen previously.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:42.720
Riya said, mark it complete.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:47.130
Agent translated that into the
right state code, status code,

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:48.960
on the right activity.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:53.100
No status picklists, no
activities, subgrids,

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:56.280
or anything else
to scroll through.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:58.170
Carlos, the sales manager,

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:01.080
made a call to Hard
Coffee this morning.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:03.330
Watch everything
the agent infers

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:06.150
from this single
sentence in the prompt.

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:10.290
Create a phone call activity
on the Hard Coffee account.

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:13.560
Carlos spoke with the
procurement lead, Daniela,

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.900
about the annual bean contract.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:19.680
She wants samples of the
new Ethiopia Guji lot

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:21.210
before signing.

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:23.850
Look at what the agent inferred.

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:26.490
Activity type as phone call.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:29.790
It linked the account
via regarding object ID,

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:33.750
marked it as completed because
the call has already happened.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:37.680
Said the owner to Carlos
because it was his call.

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.590
Riya is logging
it on his behalf.

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:44.640
And it spotted Daniela on
the account's contacts

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:47.250
and linked her as a participant.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:50.160
45 minutes of Thursday
afternoon collapsed

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:52.410
into 5 minutes of intent.

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:55.350
Same plugin Maya used,
different operator,

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:58.740
different job, same pattern.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:02.070
We can't forget about
administrators as they want

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:04.050
to automate their
workloads as well.

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:07.680
Kent, what can you
tell about how Amara,

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:11.970
as our platform admin, uses the
same Dataverse plugin to help

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:14.220
with her responsibilities?

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:14.730
KENT WEARE: Sure.

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:17.370
Amara is doing some really
interesting things by being able

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:20.940
to describe and create
sophisticated security models

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:22.680
using natural language.

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:26.250
Once the security models are in
place, Amara is able to go ahead

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:29.670
and validate them as well.

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:32.250
Amara is about to mutate roles,

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:34.710
business units and
field security.

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:37.800
She wants the agent to confirm
her privileges before offering

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:39.630
to do anything destructive.

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:41.520
She will connect to her
desired environment

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:43.710
by issuing the following prompt.

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:46.140
Connect me to the Zava
production environment.

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:47.850
I'll be making security changes,

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:51.420
so verify I have system
administrative privileges before

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:53.040
we start.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.050
Now, two things worth noticing.

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:58.110
First, we had the same
install of the plugin.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:00.780
No administrative-specific
bootstrap.

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:04.740
Second, the agent verified
Amara's role before proceeding.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.860
A less privileged user would
have seen exactly what they're

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:10.080
missing in plain English.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:11.850
That's the kind of
pre-flight check

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:16.740
that prevents half-finished
security changes from occurring.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:18.360
Now, let's get into it.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:21.750
Amara describes the security
plan the way she would describe

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:23.580
it to a junior admin.

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:27.150
Business units, roles,
field security,

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:30.210
access team templates,
and user assignments.

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:33.450
And the agent will fan
out across six subsystems

00:13:33.450 --> 00:13:35.340
in order to go build it.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:39.840
Here's the prompt that was used
in order to address these needs.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:41.490
As you can see,
it's very detailed,

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:44.040
but nothing our
plugin can't handle.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:48.720
We're rolling out to two regions
and three job functions,

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:52.140
setting up the following
business units for Portland

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:56.370
and Seattle as child business
units underneath the route.

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.830
Now, we're going to go ahead
and create some custom roles.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:01.050
We're going to create
a Zava sales rep role

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:03.300
with specific entity access.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:06.630
We're also going to go ahead
and create Zava warehouse op

00:14:06.630 --> 00:14:09.390
with access to the
operational entities.

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:11.580
A Zava leadership
role that has read

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:14.940
on everything org-wide,
but no writes.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:17.700
And then also introduce
field-level security.

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.520
So we'll restrict the
account.lifetime value

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:25.080
so only system administrators
and Zava leadership can read it.

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:29.160
Now, we'll go ahead and
access team templates

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:33.420
and use the cross-region account
collaborators on account,

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:35.820
read, and append to rights.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:39.150
For assignments, let's go
ahead and assign Dakota

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:42.120
to the Portland business
unit as a sales rep.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:44.640
Let's then go ahead
and assign Lisa

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:48.570
to the Seattle business
unit as a Zava sales rep.

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:50.790
And we'll go ahead and add Mario

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:55.680
to the Portland business unit
Zava warehouse op group.

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:58.290
Now, of course, we want to
make sure we put everything

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:02.280
into a solution
called Zava security.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:05.640
Now, watch what the agent
did without being asked.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.070
It enabled the column
for field security

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:11.310
at the schema level before
creating the profile.

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:15.420
The prerequisite people forget
that produces no audit entries

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:16.800
and no error messages.

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:18.120
That's a problem.

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:20.550
It assigned the field-level
security profile

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:22.230
to specific roles, though,

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:27.420
because it created an unassigned
profile and did nothing.

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:29.700
And it added every
security component

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:32.610
to the solution
explicitly, roles,

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:35.910
field-level security
profiles, team templates.

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:39.990
Those don't auto-add the
way these tables did.

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:43.890
Since configuration without
validation is hope,

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:47.220
Amara wants to confirm for
each user what they can

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:50.640
and cannot see, in line
without juggling multiple

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:52.080
browser sessions.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:55.470
She'll issue a prompt of
validate the security model

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.740
for each of Dakota,
Lisa, and Mario.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:01.320
Simulate their
access and report.

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:03.270
Can they read an account record?

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:05.400
Can they see a lifetime value?

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:07.800
Can they read a
roast batch record?

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.740
Can they read each other's
business unit records?

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:14.700
Show me a clear pass-fail table.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:18.270
As we can see, we have all
15 security validation tests

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:19.380
that have passed.

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:21.660
Green check marks,
where they need to be.

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.000
Red X's where they
also need to be.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:26.790
The agent did inline what
usually means logging

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:31.050
in as each user in a
private browser tab.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:35.640
Configuration has been confirmed
and done so in seconds.

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.980
So now we have
another requirement.

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:39.300
Lisa needs access

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:42.600
to the Portland coffee
account, but Dakota owns it.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:45.930
So Amara is going to go
ahead and issue a prompt,

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:47.700
and the reason for this
is that they're going

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:50.610
to be co-pitching a
Seattle expansion

00:16:50.610 --> 00:16:52.680
to that particular cafe.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:54.720
So we need to share one record

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:59.640
with Lisa via the cross-region
account collaborators team.

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:01.710
Now, this is still
a controlled path.

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:05.040
We have Lisa from
Seattle's business unit,

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:07.260
and Lisa needs read access

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:10.110
on that one Portland
coffee shop account

00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:12.180
that Dakota happens to own.

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:13.620
Because they're co-pitching,

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:17.300
we need to enable the
right level of sharing.

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:20.210
Notice what the agent didn't do.

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:24.170
It didn't ask Amara for a GUID
for a particular account.

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:27.950
No GUID for Lisa, no long-form
description of access rights.

00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:32.580
Those came from the team
template she defined earlier.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:37.110
One line of intent, one row of
principal object access written,

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:41.220
the cross-region
collaboration is unblocked.

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:44.940
Amara has another requirement
related to auditing,

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:47.820
and she'll issue a prompt
of enable auditing

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:52.290
on account.lifetime value so
that we have a log of every read

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:56.010
and write, including who
accessed it and when,

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:59.580
then confirm the audit
setting is active.

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:01.920
Naturally, auditing
is rather important.

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:04.860
Let's see what happens when
Amara turns on auditing

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:08.340
for a sensitive column
so that future reads

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:12.240
and writes land in
the log correctly.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:16.110
Now, auditing in Dataverse
is a three-layer system.

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:20.310
We've got orgs,
tables, and columns,

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:24.390
and missing one layer produces
no entries with no errors.

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:25.860
That's a problem.

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:29.220
The agent, however, though,
verifies each layer

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:33.600
and only then turns the
column level setting on.

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:34.710
Now, every change

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:38.550
to the lifetime value
lands in the audit log.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:42.690
In five minutes, Amara stood
up two business units,

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:46.860
three custom roles, a field
security profile assigned,

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:50.610
an access team template,
three user assignments,

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:54.360
validated the whole
model by impersonation,

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:57.180
and set up a cross-region share.

00:18:57.180 --> 00:19:00.240
Every piece lives
inside of a solution

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:03.900
that she can now
version and redeploy.

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:08.940
Extremely powerful capabilities
for our admin personas.

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:11.070
SUYESH KSHIRSAGAR: Well,
that concludes our session.

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:14.850
We saw three different roles,
three completely different jobs,

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:16.770
but one unified approach.

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:19.200
If any of you are
interested in learning more

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:22.920
about the capabilities that were
demonstrated or get your hands

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:24.860
on the Dataverse
plugin yourself,

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:28.400
please scan the appropriate
QR code on your screen.

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:30.800
KENT WEARE: Thank you so much
for joining us here today.

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:32.610
SUYESH KSHIRSAGAR:
Thank you, indeed.
